“Venerable sir, there was once a *brāhmaṇa* who was old, aged, and burdened with years, and he had as a wife a young brahmin girl (*māṇavikā*) who was pregnant and near her confinement.

Then she told him: ‘Go, *brāhmaṇa*, buy a young monkey (*makkaṭacchāpaka*) in the market (*āpaṇa*) and bring it back to me as a playmate (*kīḷāpanaka*) for my child (*kumāraka*).’

He replied: ‘Wait, madam (*bhoti*), till you have borne the child.’

If you bear a boy (*kumāraka*), then I will go to the market (*āpaṇa*) and buy a young male monkey (*makkaṭacchāpaka*) and bring it back to you as a playmate (*kīḷāpanaka*) for your little boy (*kumāraka*);

but if you bear a girl (*kumārika*), then I will go to the market (*āpaṇa*) and buy a young female monkey (*makkaṭacchāpika*) and bring it back to you as a playmate (*kīḷāpanika*) for your little girl.’

For the second time, she made the same request and received the same answer. For the third time, she made the same request.

Then, since his mind was bound to her with love (*sāratto paṭibaddhacitto*), he went to the market (*āpaṇa*), bought a young male monkey (*makkaṭacchāpaka*), brought it back, and told her: ‘I have bought this young male monkey (*makkaṭacchāpaka*) in the market (*āpaṇa*) and brought it back to you as a playmate (*kīḷāpanaka*) for your child (*kumāraka*).’

Then she told him: ‘Go, *brāhmaṇa*, take this young male monkey (*makkaṭacchāpaka*) to *Rattapāṇi* the dyer’s son (*rajataputto*) and tell him: “Good *Rattapāṇi*, I want this young male monkey (*makkaṭacchāpaka*) dyed the colour called yellow-unguent (*pītāvalepana*), pounded and re-pounded (*ākoṭitapaccākoṭita*), and smoothened out on both sides (*ubhatobhāgavimaṭṭha*.”’

Then, since his mind was bound to her with love (*sāratto paṭibaddhacitto*), he took the young male monkey (*makkaṭacchāpaka*) to *Rattapāṇi* the dyer’s son (*rajakaputto*) and told him: ‘Good *Rattapāṇi*, I want this young male monkey (*makkaṭacchāpaka*) dyed the colour called yellow-unguent (*pītāvalepana*), pounded and re-pounded (*ākoṭitapaccākoṭita*), and smoothened out on both sides (*ubhatobhāgavimaṭṭha*.’

*Rattapāṇi* the dyer’s son told him: ‘Venerable sir, this young male monkey (*makkaṭacchāpaka*) will take a dyeing but not a pounding or a smoothening out.’

So too, the doctrine (*vāda*) of the fools is fit for dyeing, but the doctrine of the wise, the *Paṇḍitas*, will withstand testing and being smoothened out.

Then, on another occasion that *brāhmaṇa* took a pair of new garments to *Rattapāṇi* the dyer’s son (*rajakaputto*) and told him: ‘Good *Rattapāṇi*, I want this pair of new garments dyed the colour called yellow-unguent (*pītāvalepana*), pounded and re-pounded (*ākoṭitapaccākoṭita*), and smoothened out on both sides (*ubhatobhāgavimaṭṭha*.’

*Rattapāṇi* the dyer’s son told him: ‘Venerable sir, this pair of new garments will take a dyeing and a pounding and a smoothening out.’

So too, the doctrine (*vāda*) of that Blessed One (*bhagavato*), accomplished (*arahato*) and fully enlightened (*sammāsambuddha*), will give delight to the wise (*paṇḍitānaṃ*) but not to fools (*bālānaṃ*), and it will withstand testing and being smoothened out.”

“Householder (*gahapati*), the assembly (*parisā*) together with its rulers (*sarājikā*) knows thus: ‘Upāli, the householder (*gahapati*), is a disciple (*sāvako*) of *Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta*.’

Whose disciple (*sāvaka*) do they take you to be, householder (*gahapati*)?” When this had been said, Upāli the householder (*gahapati*) rose from his seat, arranged his upper robe (*uttarāsaṅga*) over one shoulder, saluted the Blessed One (*bhagavā*) with joined hands (*añjaliṃ paṇāmetvā*) and said to *Nigaṇṭha Nāṭaputta*: “In that case, venerable sir (*bhante*), listen to whose disciple (*sāvako*) I am” —

“Of the steadfast, rid of delusion (*vigatamohassa*), whose stake is broken (*pabhinnakhīlassa*), victor in victory (*vijitavijayassa*),

free from anguish (*anīghassa*), whose mind is very even (*susamacittassa*), of developed virtue (*vuddhasīlassa*), possessed of excellent wisdom (*sādhupaññassa*),

of *Vessantara*, spotless (*vimalassa*), of that Blessed One (*bhagavato*), I am a disciple (*sāvakohamasmi*).

“Free from doubt (*akathaṃkathissa*), delighted (*tusitassa*), having rejected worldly pleasures (*vantalokāmisassa*), gladdened (*muditassa*),

who has completed his religious exercises (*katasamaṇassa*), of the man (*manujassa*) whose last body (*antimasārīrassa*) this is, of that man (*narassa*),

of the peerless (*anopamassa*), spotless (*virajassa*), of that Blessed One (*bhagavato*), I am a disciple (*sāvakohamasmi*).

“Of the one free from uncertainty (*asaṃsayassa*), skilled (*kusalassa*), the guide (*venayikassa*), the excellent charioteer (*sārathivarassa*),

of the unsurpassed (*anuttarassa*), of the lovely *Dhamma*, without doubt (*nikkaṅkhassa*), illuminating…pī.
