119. “Rāhula, develop meditation that is like the earth;
for when you develop meditation that is like the earth, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.
Just as people throw clean things and dirty things, excrement, urine, spittle, pus, and blood on the earth, and the earth is not horrified, humiliated, and disgusted because of that, so too, Rāhula, develop meditation that is like the earth;
for when you develop meditation that is like the earth, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.

“Rāhula, develop meditation that is like water;
for when you develop meditation that is like water, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.
Just as people wash clean things and dirty things, excrement, urine, spittle, pus, and blood in water, and the water is not horrified, humiliated, and disgusted because of that, so too, Rāhula, develop meditation that is like water;
for when you develop meditation that is like water, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.

“Rāhula, develop meditation that is like fire;
for when you develop meditation that is like fire, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.
Just as fire burns clean things and dirty things, excrement, urine, spittle, pus, and blood, and the fire is not horrified, humiliated, and disgusted because of that, so too, Rāhula, develop meditation that is like fire;
for when you develop meditation that is like fire, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.

“Rāhula, develop meditation that is like air;
for when you develop meditation that is like air, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.
Just as the air blows on clean things and dirty things, on excrement, urine, spittle, pus, and blood, and the air is not horrified, humiliated, and disgusted because of that, so too, Rāhula, develop meditation that is like air;
for when you develop meditation that is like air, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.

“Rāhula, develop meditation that is like space;
for when you develop meditation that is like space, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.
Just as space is not established anywhere, so too, Rāhula, develop meditation that is like space;
for when you develop meditation that is like space, arisen agreeable and disagreeable contacts will not invade your mind and remain.

253. “Furthermore, Udayin, I have proclaimed to my disciples the way, practicing which my disciples can create from this body another body (kāya) having form (rūpiṃ), mind-made (manomayaṃ), with all its limbs (sabbaṅgapaccaṅgiṃ), lacking no faculty (ahīnindriyaṃ).

Just as, Udayin, a man were to pull out a reed (īsikaṃ) from its sheath (muñjamhā) and think thus: ‘This is the sheath (muñjo), this is the reed (īsikā); the sheath (muñjo) is one, the reed (īsikā) is another; it is from the sheath (muñjamhā) that the reed (īsikā) has been pulled out’;

or just as, Udayin, a man were to pull out a sword (asiṃ) from its scabbard (kosiyā) and think thus: ‘This is the sword (asi), this is the scabbard (kosi); the sword (asi) is one, the scabbard (kosi) another; it is from the scabbard (kosiyā) that the sword (asi) has been pulled out’;

or just as, Udayin, a man were to pull a snake (ahiṃ) out of its basket (karaṇḍā) and think thus: ‘This is the snake (ahi), this is the basket (karaṇḍo); the snake (ahi) is one, the basket (karaṇḍo) another; it is from the basket (karaṇḍā) that the snake (ahi) has been pulled out.’

So too, Udayin, I have proclaimed to my disciples the way, practicing which my disciples create from this body (kāyā) another body (kāyaṃ) having form (rūpiṃ), mind-made (manomayaṃ), complete in all its parts (sabbaṅgapaccaṅgiṃ), not lacking any faculties (ahīnindriyaṃ).

And thereby many disciples of mine abide having reached the consummation and perfection of direct knowledge (abhiññāvosānapāramippattā).

302. Then Venerable Raṭṭhapāla, having eaten, with his bowl washed, standing, spoke these verses:

"Behold this painted image (bimbaṃ), a body (arukāyaṃ) set up,

subject to sickness, full of intentions, wherein there is no lasting stability (ṭhiti)."

"Behold this painted form (rūpaṃ), with jewels (maṇinā) and earrings (kuṇḍalena);

bones (aṭṭhi) covered with skin (tacena), it appears beautiful with clothes (vatthebhi)."

"The feet (pādā) are stained with red dye (alattakakatā), the face (mukhaṃ) is smeared with powder (cuṇṇakamakkhitaṃ);

enough (alaṃ) to delude (mohāya) the fool (bālassa), but not the seeker of the further shore (pāragavesino)."

"The hair (kesā) is adorned (aṭṭhāpadakatā), the eyes (nettā) are smeared with collyrium (añjanamakkhitā);

enough to delude the fool, but not the seeker of the further shore."

"Like a newly painted unguent pot (añjanīva navā cittā), a putrid body (pūtikāyo) adorned;

enough to deceive a fool (bālassa mohāya), but not a seeker for the further shore (pāragavesino)."

"The deer-hunter (migavo) set out the snare (pāsaṃ), but the deer (migo) did not spring the trap (vākaraṃ);

having eaten the bait (nivāpaṃ), we shall go (gacchāma), while the deer-hunter (migabandhake) weeps (kandante)."

Then Venerable Raṭṭhapāla, having spoken these verses while standing, went to the Migacira garden of King Koravya; having arrived, he sat down at the root of a certain tree for the day's abiding (divāvihāraṃ).

307. I see people in the world who have wealth, but through delusion, they do not give.

Greedy, they stash away wealth, and long all the more for sensual pleasures.

The king, having forcefully conquered the earth, dwells in majesty with the ocean as its border;

unsatisfied with this shore of the ocean, he longs for the far shore as well.

Kings and many other men, their thirst unquenched, approach death;

like those who are deficient, they abandon the body; there is no satisfaction of sensual pleasures in the world.

Their relatives lament, scattering their hair, crying, "Alas, they are not immortal!"

Having carried him out, covered with a cloth, and placed him on a pyre, they then set him on fire.

He is burned, prodded with stakes, having left behind his possessions with a single cloth;

for the one dying, there are no protectors, neither relatives here, nor friends, nor companions.

Heirs take away his wealth; but beings go according to their actions (karma).

Nothing whatsoever follows the one who is dying: neither children, nor wives, nor wealth, nor territory.

With wealth, one does not obtain a long life, nor with riches does one ward off old age;

"Brief is this life," say the wise, "impermanent, subject to change."

Both rich and poor experience contact; both fool and wise are touched in the same way;

the fool, because of foolishness, lies pierced, as if struck down; but the wise person does not tremble when touched by contact.

Therefore, wisdom is better than wealth, through which one attains the cessation of suffering in this life;

because of not clinging, evil deeds are done through delusion.

They approach both the womb and another world, undergoing rebirth in succession;

those of little wisdom, believing, approach both the womb and another world.

Just as a thief is seized at the break-in, so the evil-doer is struck down by his own actions;

likewise, people, having passed away in the next world, are struck down by their own actions.
"Indeed, desires are varied, sweet, and delightful, they stir the mind with their diverse forms; having seen the danger in sensual pleasures, that’s why, O king, I went forth.

Just as fruits fall from trees, so do humans, young and old alike, at the breaking of the body; having seen this also, O king, I went forth, … Best is the Monk’s State Incontrovertible."

The Raṭṭhapāla Sutta, the second [sutta], is concluded.

341.

Then, Prince, this occurred to me: ‘To whom should I first teach the Dharma? Who will quickly understand this Dharma?’ Then, Prince, this occurred to me: ‘The group of five monks (*pañcavaggiyā bhikkhū*) were very helpful to me, as they attended to me when I was resolute in exertion. Suppose I taught the Dhamma first to the group of five monks (*pañcavaggiyānaṃ bhikkhūnaṃ*) .’ Then, Prince, this occurred to me: ‘Where are the bhikkhus of the group of five (*pañcavaggiyā bhikkhū*) now living?’

And with the divine eye (*dibbena cakkhunā*), which is purified and surpasses the human (*atikkantamānusakena*), I saw that the group of five monks (*pañcavaggiye bhikkhū*) were staying near Bārāṇasī (*bārāṇasiyaṃ*), in the Deer Park at Isipatana (*isipatane migadāye*). Then, Prince, after staying at Uruvelā (*uruvelāyaṃ*) for as long as he liked, I set out wandering toward Benares (*bārāṇasī*).

Then he saw me, Prince, the Ājīvaka Upaka (*upako ājīvako*) between Gayā (*gayaṃ*) and the Bodhi tree (*bodhiṃ*), on the road.

Seeing me, he said this: ‘Your faculties, friend, are very clear (*vippasannāni indriyāni*), purified is your skin and bright (*parisuddho chavivaṇṇo pariyodāto*). Who are you, friend, having gone forth intending what? Who is your teacher (*satthā*)? Whose Dharma (*dhammaṃ*) do you approve of?’ When this was said, I, Prince, addressed the Ājīvaka Upaka (*upakaṃ ājīvakaṃ*) with these verses:

‘I am the conqueror of all, the knower of all (*sabbābhibhū sabbavidūhamasmi*),

I am untainted in all things (*sabbesu dhammesu anūpalitto*);

I have abandoned all, I am freed in the destruction of craving (*taṇhākkhaye vimutto*),

myself having understood, whom should I point out?

‘I have no teacher (*ācariyo atthi*), there is no one equal to me (*sadiso me na vijjati*);

in the world with its gods, there is no rival for me (*natthi me paṭipuggalo*).

‘For I am the Arhat (*arahā*) in the world, I am the unsurpassed teacher (*satthā anuttaro*);

I alone am a fully awakened Buddha (*sammāsambuddho*), cooled and extinguished (*sītibhūtosmi nibbuto*).

‘To set in motion the wheel of the Dharma (*dhammacakkaṃ pavattetuṃ*), I am going to the town of Kāsī (*kāsinaṃ puraṃ*);

in a world that has become blind (*andhībhūtasmiṃ lokasmiṃ*), I go to beat the drum of the Deathless (*amatadundubhi*) .’

‘Just as you declare, friend, you are the Arhat (*arahasi*), the endless conqueror (*anantajino*) .’

‘Like me are the conquerors (*jinā honti*), who have attained the destruction of the āsavas (*āsavakkhayaṃ*);

I have conquered evil things (*pāpakā dhammā*), therefore, Upaka, I am a conqueror (*jino’ti*) .’
