349. Then the thief Aṅgulimāla addressed the Blessed One in a verse:

“While going, you say you are stopped, *samaṇa*;
And you say that I am stopped, yet not stopped;
I ask you, *samaṇa*, about this matter,
How are you stopped, and I not stopped?”

“I am always stopped, Aṅgulimāla,
Having laid down the club for all beings;
But you are unrestrained in your hands;
Therefore, I am stopped, and you are not stopped.”

“After a long time, indeed, the great sage, revered by me,
The truthful speaker, has entered the great forest .

I will go about having abandoned evil,
Having heard the verse connected with your *dharma*.”

Thus, indeed, the thief cast the sword and weapon
Into a pit, a precipice, a hell;
The thief bowed to the feet of the Well-Gone,
And there he asked for ordination.

The Buddha was compassionate and a great sage,
The teacher of the world with its gods;
Then he said, ‘Come, *bhikkhu*’;
That alone was his state of being a *bhikkhu*.

394. Then, indeed, the Blessed One replied to the brāhmaṇa Brahmāyu with these verses:

“He who knows the former dwelling, and sees heaven and the realms of loss;

and has attained the ending of birth, the sage, accomplished in deep knowledge (abhijñā),

knows the mind as pure, altogether freed from passions;

he has abandoned birth and death, is perfect in the holy life (brahmacariya);

gone to the beyond in all things (sabbadhammānaṃ), such a Buddha is proclaimed."

When this was said, the brāhmaṇa Brahmāyu rose from his seat, and after arranging his upper robe on one shoulder, he prostrated himself with his head at the Blessed One’s feet, and he covered the Blessed One’s feet with kisses and caressed them with his hands, pronouncing his name: “I am the brāhmaṇa Brahmāyu, Master Gotama; I am the brāhmaṇa Brahmāyu, Master Gotama.”

Then, indeed, that assembly became one with astonished and amazed minds: “It is wonderful, indeed, sir! It is amazing, indeed, sir! That this brāhmaṇa Brahmāyu, known and famous, will make such an act of extreme reverence!”

Then, indeed, the Blessed One said this to the brāhmaṇa Brahmāyu: “Enough, brāhmaṇa, arise; sit down in your own seat since your mind has confidence in me.”

Then, indeed, the brāhmaṇa Brahmāyu rose and sat down in his own seat.
